<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Alternative visualization of the original set of 8 “master
key filters”, extracted by Babaiee <span id="S1.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span>
(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025a</a></cite>), while here complemented
with a normalization the filters, (i) by rescaling Filters 1–6 to
give the same response to matching first-order discrete monomials under
discrete convolution
as for convolution of the corresponding continuous monomials with corresponding
continuous Gaussian derivative operators,
according to (<a href="#S2.E63" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E68" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>), and
(ii) by adding different constants to Filters 7–8, according to
(<a href="#S2.E69" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">69</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E73" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">73</span></a>),
to minimize the variance-based spatial spread measure
of the filters, and finally visualizing the data on a blue-red colour scale with the
value 0 corresponding to white, to better reveal the
polarities of the filter values.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
