<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S1.F3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Idealized models of the 8 “master key filters” in
Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, in terms of combinations pure
difference operators with spatial smoothing filters based on the
discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel, with the amount of
spatial smoothing determined from the spatial extents of the 8
“master key filters” using <span id="S1.F3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method A</span> in
Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, based on <span id="S1.F3.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">direct transfer of scale
values from the variances for a continuous Gaussian derivative
model</span>.
(Idealized 1): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S1.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 2): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S1.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{y,2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{y,2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 3): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S1.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{y,3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{y,3})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 4): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S1.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{y,4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{y,4})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 5): Centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S1.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{y,5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{y,5})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 6): Centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S1.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{y,6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{y,6})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 7): Sharpening operation
<math id="S1.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 8): Pure spatial smoothing
<math id="S1.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{y,8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{y,8})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(The numerical values of the scale parameters <math id="S1.F3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.F3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y,i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.F3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> have been determined according
to Table <a href="#S3.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Note: ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>,
and with <math id="S1.F3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> additionally according to
Table <a href="#S3.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 3.6 Method D2: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.)
(Note that the contrasts of Filters 2 and 3 are reversed
in relation to
the sign conventions for the corresponding “master key filters”.)</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, Babaiee <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024b</a></cite>,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024a</a></cite>,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025a</a></cite>)
have investigated the properties of
depthwise convolutional filters learned across all layers of depthwise-separable
convolutional neural networks, as originally proposed by
Howard <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">2017</a></cite>),
with particular focus on the ConvNeXt architectures by Liu <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span>
(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">2022</a></cite>).
Through unsupervised clustering techniques applied to
millions of trained filters, they discovered that depthwise convolutional kernels
can be categorized into a few distinct classes resembling Gaussian functions and their
derivatives. Remarkably, these filter patterns demonstrate generality regardless of layer
depth, network architecture, or training data domain. Through systematic greedy
search methods, they further demonstrated that this diverse set of learned filters can
without major loss of performance be reduced to a smaller set of just 8 so-called
“master key filters”,
see Figures <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
for two different ways of
illustrating the same data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, these receptive fields do specifically appear to be qualitatively very
similar to receptive fields obtained from discrete scale-space theory,
in terms of either (see
Figure <a href="#S1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>
for an illustration):</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">anti-symmetric first-order derivative approximations at moderately fine scales (Filters 5 and 6),</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a Gaussian blob at a moderately fine scale (Filter 8),</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a local sharpening operation by subtracting a
Laplacian-of-the-Gaussian
at a moderately fine scale (Filter 7), or</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">non-centered asymmetric first-order derivative approximations at very fine scales (Filters 1–4).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The subject of this paper is to present a quantitative analysis of these learned filters, and specifically to make comparisons to idealized simplifications with different degrees of simplification in idealized models for spatial receptive fields, based on an earlier presented theory for discrete derivative approximations with scale-space properties in Lindeberg (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">1993a</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">1993b</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the results to be presented will show that the learned
filters in depthwise-separable deep networks based on the ConvNeXt
architecture can be well modelled by discrete scale-space filters.
By quantitative results regarding deep learning, we also show that
replacement of the learned filter by idealized scale-space filters in
depthwise-separable deep networks also lead to almost as good results as
when using the fully learned filters. In these respects, the results
presented in this paper generalize the previous solid theoretical
foundation of using Gaussian derivative filters as the first layer of
linear filters in a vision system to also using (here discrete
approximations of) Gaussian derivative operators in the higher layers
of deep networks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Given that the ConvNeXt architecture has been
demonstrated to constitute a modern architecture for deep networks
with very good ability to compete with Transformer architectures,
a main significance of this work is that it establishes a strong link between
the filters learned in a reduced ConvNeXt architecture and the
idealized filters predicted from theoretically based scale-space theory.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Structure of this paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The presentation is organized as follows:
Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> starts by describing a
theoretical background for measuring characteristic properties of the
learned filters, based on spatial spread measures defined from
spatial means and spatial variances of the
absolute values of the filter coefficients and the responses to
lower-order polynomials of the learned filters.
Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> then performs modelling of
the 8 “master key filters” using four different types of main
methods in terms of:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Method A: Spatial spread measures as matched between discrete spatial spread
measures of the learned filters and continuous spatial spread
measures of continuous Gaussian derivatives.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Method B: Spatial spread measures as matched between discrete spatial spread
measures of the learned filters and discrete spatial spread
measures of discrete scale-space kernels defined by applying small
support difference operators to the discrete analogue of the
Gaussian kernel.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Method C: Minimization of the difference between the learned filters
and idealized discrete scale-space filters in discrete <math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Method D: Minimization of the difference between the learned filters
and idealized discrete scale-space filters in discrete <math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Interpretation of the analysis and modelling results in terms of scale-space theory for spatial receptive fields ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> first interprets the results obtained in
terms of the spaces spanned by the receptive field responses in terms
of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-jet representations in terms of Gaussian derivatives as well as
possible implications for the construction of more general Gaussian
derivative networks, with the layers defined from linear combination
of Gaussian derivative responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experimental results ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> then applies the fitted idealized
discrete scale-space filters as the filtering primitives in
depthwise-separable networks applied to the ImageNet dataset,
and shows that (i) the idealized filters based on Method B have the
predictive properties out of the four main classes of filter modelling
methods, and (ii) using the idealized models of the
8 “master key filters” as the computational primitives in
depthwise-separable deep networks lead to almost as good results as using
the original learned filters.
Finally, Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Summary and discussion ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> concludes with a summary and
discussion of some of the main results.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key
filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to
lower-order polynomials</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will apply a similar methodology, as used for modelling
and characterizing the discrete derivative approximation methods in
Lindeberg (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025</a></cite>),
to model the 8 “master key filters” extracted by
Babaiee <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025a</a></cite>),
while also employing structurally closely related methods for determining
the parameters of idealized receptive field models, as previously used
for matching the parameters between different types of theoretical
models for temporal receptive fields in
Lindeberg (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">2023</a></cite>, Sections 3.3–3.4).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For measuring the overall effect of a non-negative receptive field <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(p)=h(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(p)=h(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>
with the image coordinates <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=(x,y)^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=(x,y)^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with
regard to its spatial extent, it is natural to formulate
a spatial spread measure in terms of the spatial mean <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(h(\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(h(\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math>
and the spatial variance <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(h(\cdot,\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(h(\cdot,\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math> according to</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}M(h(\cdot))&amp;=\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,dp},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}M(h(\cdot))&amp;=\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,dp},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}V(h(\cdot))&amp;=\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,p^{T}\,h(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,dp}-\left(\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,dp}\right)^{2},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}V(h(\cdot))&amp;=\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,p^{T}\,h(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,dp}-\left(\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,dp}\right)^{2},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">over a continuous image domain and according to</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}M(h(\cdot))&amp;=\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}M(h(\cdot))&amp;=\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}V(h(\cdot))&amp;=\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,p^{T}\,h(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)}-\left(\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)}\right)^{2},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}V(h(\cdot))&amp;=\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,p^{T}\,h(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)}-\left(\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)}\right)^{2},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">over a discrete image domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In previous work, the use of such spatial spread
measure has been demonstrated to have a predictive ability to
compare the effective amount of spatial smoothing in receptive fields,
when performing learning of the scale levels in Gaussian
derivative networks using different types
of discretization operations for Gaussian derivative operators,
see Perzanowski and Lindeberg (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.1 </span>Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order scale-space kernels</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the continuous 2-D Gaussian kernel</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g(x,y;\;\sigma)=g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma)\,g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(y;\;\sigma)=\frac{1}{2\pi\sigma^{2}}\,e^{-(x^{2}+y^{2})/2\sigma^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">;</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">;</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.130em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">;</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x,y;\;\sigma)=g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma)\,g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(y;\;\sigma)=\frac{1}{2\pi\sigma^{2}}\,e^{-(x^{2}+y^{2})/2\sigma^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with the 1-D Gaussian kernel given by</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma}\,e^{-x^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">;</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}\sigma}\,e^{-x^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with standard deviation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and the spatial variance <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the resulting variance-based spread measure is then</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma))=\sigma^{2}\,I," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma))=\sigma^{2}\,I,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the unit matrix.
Similarly, for the 2-D discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel
according to
(Lindeberg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">1990</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024</a></cite>)</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(m,n;\;\sigma)=T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(m;\;\sigma)\,T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(n;\;\sigma)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(m,n;\;\sigma)=T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(m;\;\sigma)\,T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(n;\;\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">with the 1-D discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel given by</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(n;\;\sigma)=e^{-s}I_{n}(\sigma^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(n;\;\sigma)=e^{-s}I_{n}(\sigma^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}(\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}(\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the modified Bessel functions of integer order
(see Abramowitz and Stegun <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1964</a></cite>),
the corresponding discrete spatial spread measure is correspondingly
(Lindeberg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">1993a</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024</a></cite>)</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma))=\sigma^{2}\,I." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma))=\sigma^{2}\,I.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.7" class="ltx_p">Since both the continuous Gaussian kernel and the discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel are centered, we also have that the spatial mean values are</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}M(g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma))=0,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}M(g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma))=0,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}M(T(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma))=0.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}M(T(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma))=0.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.2 </span>Spatial spread measures applied to first-order scale-space kernels</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the methodology in Lindeberg (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025</a></cite>),
we will in this paper also perform corresponding characterizations of receptive fields
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> that are not guaranteed to be non-negative, by then measuring the mean value
and the variance of the absolute value of the receptive field <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|h(x,y)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|h(x,y)|</annotation></semantics></math>,
in terms of the entities <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By computing these spatial spread measures for the horizontal first-order Gaussian derivative kernel</p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{x}(x,y;\;\sigma)=\partial_{x}(g(x,y;\;\sigma))=-\frac{x}{\sigma^{2}}\,g(x,y;\;\sigma)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{x}(x,y;\;\sigma)=\partial_{x}(g(x,y;\;\sigma))=-\frac{x}{\sigma^{2}}\,g(x,y;\;\sigma),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">we obtain</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}M(|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}M(|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}V(|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}2\sigma^{2}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\sigma^{2}\end{array}\right).\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}V(|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}2\sigma^{2}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\sigma^{2}\end{array}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Similarly, for the vertical first-order Gaussian derivative kernel</p>
<table id="S2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{y}(x,y;\;\sigma)=\partial_{y}(g(x,y;\;\sigma))=-\frac{y}{\sigma^{2}}\,g(x,y;\;\sigma)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{y}(x,y;\;\sigma)=\partial_{y}(g(x,y;\;\sigma))=-\frac{y}{\sigma^{2}}\,g(x,y;\;\sigma),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">it holds that</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}M(|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}M(|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}V(|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\sigma^{2}&amp;0\\
0&amp;2\sigma^{2}\end{array}\right).\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}V(|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\sigma^{2}&amp;0\\
0&amp;2\sigma^{2}\end{array}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Later, we will make use these results in combination with
computed discrete variances of discrete kernels,
that resemble first-order derivative approximations,
for estimating the scale parameter <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in corresponding
idealized models of receptive fields in terms of scale-space operations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="S2.F4.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S2.F4.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.F4.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.F4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{x+}=\left(\begin{array}[]{ccc}0&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;-1&amp;+1\\
0&amp;0&amp;0\end{array}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{x+}=\left(\begin{array}[]{ccc}0&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;-1&amp;+1\\
0&amp;0&amp;0\end{array}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.F4.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{x-}=\left(\begin{array}[]{ccc}0&amp;0&amp;0\\
-1&amp;+1&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0\end{array}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{x-}=\left(\begin{array}[]{ccc}0&amp;0&amp;0\\
-1&amp;+1&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0\end{array}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.F4.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{y+}=\left(\begin{array}[]{ccc}0&amp;+1&amp;0\\
0&amp;-1&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0\end{array}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{y+}=\left(\begin{array}[]{ccc}0&amp;+1&amp;0\\
