<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The result of computing the discrete <math id="S2.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm <math id="S2.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|h(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|h(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
as well as the discrete spatial mean value <math id="S2.T1.m26" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> according to
Equation (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and the discrete spatial variance <math id="S2.T1.m27" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math>
according to Equation (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) for the 8 “master key filters”
shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Measuring the spatial extent and the spatial offset of the 8 “master key filters”</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1.3 Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order pure difference operators ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the result of computing the discrete <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm</p>
<table id="S2.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|h(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1}=\sum_{(m,n)\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}|h(m,n)|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|h(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1}=\sum_{(m,n)\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}|h(m,n)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">as well as the discrete spatial spread measures <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math>
according to Equations (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) for the 8 “master key filters”
extracted by Babaiee <span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025a</a></cite>) and shown
in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
As can be seen from these results:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all the filters, the off-diagonal elements in the spatial
covariance matrices <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> are much smaller in magnitude than the diagonal
elements. In this respect, the learned receptive field appear to be
strongly aligned with the Cartesian coordinate directions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filters 1–4, one component of the spatial mean value
<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> is very close to zero, while the other
component is rather close to either <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus reflecting a substantial deviation from
symmetry. In this respect, there is a close structural similarity to
the corresponding spatial mean values for the non-centered
first-order difference operators according to
Equations (<a href="#S2.E28" title="In 2.1.3 Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order pure difference operators ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E31" title="In 2.1.3 Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order pure difference operators ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There is, however, also a notable difference in the sense that the mean values do not very closely approximate <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, but rather <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.6</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 0.7</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filters 5–6 and Filters 7–8, the spatial mean values <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math>
are overall very close to zero,
thus indicating a strong degree of either mirror symmetry or antisymmetry.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all the Filters 1–8, the mixed component in the variance-based spatial spread measure
<math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> is very close to zero, indicating a very strong connection
of the receptive field shapes to the preferred directions of the Cartesian coordinate system.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filters 5–6, the ratio between the diagonal elements in the variance-based
spatial spread measure <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> is very close to either 2 or <math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
indicating
a rather close similarity to corresponding continuous variance-based spatial spread
measures for first-order Gaussian derivative kernels according to
Equations (<a href="#S2.E15" title="In 2.1.2 Spatial spread measures applied to first-order scale-space kernels ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S2.E18" title="In 2.1.2 Spatial spread measures applied to first-order scale-space kernels ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>).
The similarity is, however, not fully perfect, thus reflecting a minor notable difference.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filters 1–4, the ratio between the diagonal elements in the variance-based
spatial spread measure <math id="S2.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> is, however,
not in any way close to either 2 or <math id="S2.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that a direct transfer of
the corresponding expressions (<a href="#S2.E15" title="In 2.1.2 Spatial spread measures applied to first-order scale-space kernels ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S2.E18" title="In 2.1.2 Spatial spread measures applied to first-order scale-space kernels ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) for continuous first-order Gaussian derivatives
does not appear as straightforward.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filter 8, the diagonal components of the variance-based
spatial spread measure <math id="S2.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> are very similar, thus with a very good qualitative agreement
with Equations (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.1.1 Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order scale-space kernels ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E10" title="In 2.1.1 Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order scale-space kernels ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filter 7, the diagonal elements of the variance-based
spatial spread measure <math id="S2.I1.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> are somewhat similar, although
differing in a clearly notable respect.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Concerning the interpretation of in particular the variance-based spatial spread measures
for Filters 7-8, it should, however, be noted that the definitions of
these spatial spread measures assume a background magnitude value
equal to zero, while Filters 7–8 in the set of “master key filters” have
background magnitude levels that are significantly different from zero,
thereby leading to substantial bias in the spatial spread measures,
by substantially overestimating the amount of spatial extent of the filters.
Thus, some modification is needed, to be able to apply the
variance-based spatial spread measures <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> for estimating filter
parameters in idealized models to be used for modelling Filters 7–8.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural consistency requirement when constructing a centered discrete
derivative approximation operator <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math>
that is to approximate the derivative operator <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of a given order <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{+}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{+}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> over a 2-D spatial domain is to require that
the result of applying the discrete derivative approximation filter
of order <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> applied to a monomial of the same order leads to a
response that is spatially constant, with specifically</p>
<table id="S2.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}(x^{\alpha}\,y^{\beta})=\alpha!\,\beta!," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}(x^{\alpha}\,y^{\beta})=\alpha!\,\beta!,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha!=\alpha\cdot(\alpha-1)\dots 2\cdot 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha!=\alpha\cdot(\alpha-1)\dots 2\cdot 1</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the factorial of the horizontal
derivative order <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta!=\beta\cdot(\beta-1)\dots 2\cdot 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta!=\beta\cdot(\beta-1)\dots 2\cdot 1</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the factorial of
the vertical derivative order <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, for any integer monomial of order <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{+}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{+}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
if either <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;\beta</annotation></semantics></math> (or both), we should require that</p>
<table id="S2.E42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}(x^{a}\,y^{b})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}(x^{a}\,y^{b})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">These consistency requirements are similar to the results of applying corresponding
continuous pure derivative operations <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math> to corresponding monomials.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1 </span>Shift-adjusted consistency requirement for non-centered difference operators designed to approximate spatial derivative operators</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With regard to modelling the 8 “master key filters” in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,
where we have according to the analysis in connection with
Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1.3 Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order pure difference operators ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> noted that the
8 “master key filters”
(i) are not necessarily centered, and
(ii) do not decrease as rapidly with increasing distance from the center
as the discrete derivative approximation filters considered in
Lindeberg (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025</a></cite>),
 we do for the purpose
of the forthcoming analysis in this paper
generalize the criterion in Equation (<a href="#S2.E41" title="In 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>)
into the shift-adjusted consistency requirement</p>
<table id="S2.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}((x-m_{x})^{\alpha}\,(y-m_{y})^{\beta})=\alpha!\,\beta!," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}((x-m_{x})^{\alpha}\,(y-m_{y})^{\beta})=\alpha!\,\beta!,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and generalize the criterion in Equation (<a href="#S2.E42" title="In 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>)
into the shift-adjusted consistency requirement</p>
<table id="S2.E44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}((x-m_{x})^{a}\,(y-m_{y})^{b})=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}((x-m_{x})^{a}\,(y-m_{y})^{b})=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">to hold for any <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{+}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{+}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
if either <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;\beta</annotation></semantics></math> (or both),
where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the components in the spatial mean vector of the
absolute value of the discrete filter
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>, that implements the computational function of the
discrete derivative approximation operator <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math>
according to</p>
<table id="S2.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(|h_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}(\cdot,\cdot)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}m_{x}\\
m_{y}\end{array}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(|h_{x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}}(\cdot,\cdot)|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}m_{x}\\
m_{y}\end{array}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In the following analysis to be performed, we will specifically evaluate
these criteria at the centers of the responses of the discrete
derivative approximation filters to different types of
polynomials of the form <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x-m_{x})^{a}\,(y-m_{y})^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x-m_{x})^{a}\,(y-m_{y})^{b}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, these consistency requirements hold for all of the pure
non-central or central difference operators <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{x+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{x+}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{x-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{x-}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{y+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{y+}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{y-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{y-}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{xx}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{xx}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{yy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{yy}</annotation></semantics></math> that we consider in this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.2 </span>Responses to monomials for continuous Gaussian derivative operators</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the fact that the spatial scale-space representation of any image <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>,
obtained by Gaussian smoothing
expressed in terms of a parameterization of the scale parameter in terms of the
variance <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Gaussian kernel according to</p>
<table id="S2.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\cdot,\cdot;\;s)=g(\cdot,\cdot;\;s)*f(\cdot,\cdot)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\cdot,\cdot;\;s)=g(\cdot,\cdot;\;s)*f(\cdot,\cdot),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">satisfies the diffusion equation</p>
<table id="S2.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{s}L=\frac{1}{2}\,\nabla^{2}L=\frac{1}{2}\,(L_{xx}+L_{yy})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.297em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{s}L=\frac{1}{2}\,\nabla^{2}L=\frac{1}{2}\,(L_{xx}+L_{yy}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">if follows that evolution over scale for monomial input <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ab}(x,y)=x^{a}\,y^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ab}(x,y)=x^{a}\,y^{b}</annotation></semantics></math>
can be described in terms of diffusion polynomials, where we for the lowest
orders <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> have that</p>
<table id="S2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{00}(x,y;\;s)=1,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>00</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{00}(x,y;\;s)=1,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{10}(x,y;\;s)=x,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{10}(x,y;\;s)=x,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{01}(x,y;\;s)=y,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{01}(x,y;\;s)=y,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{20}(x,y;\;s)=x^{2}+s,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>20</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{20}(x,y;\;s)=x^{2}+s,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{11}(x,y;\;s)=x\,y\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.260em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{11}(x,y;\;s)=x\,y\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{02}(x,y;\;s)=y^{2}+s.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>02</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}q_{02}(x,y;\;s)=y^{2}+s.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">From this summary, we can thus read off that the Gaussian smoothing function
will leave the constant input function <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{00}(x,y)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>00</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{00}(x,y)=1</annotation></semantics></math> as well as the linear
input functions <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{10}(x,y)=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{10}(x,y)=x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{01}(x,y)=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{01}(x,y)=y</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This property will then imply that the responses of the zero-order Gaussian kernel
and its first order derivatives in the <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>- and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-directions are given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}g(\cdot,\cdot)*1&amp;=1,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}g(\cdot,\cdot)*1&amp;=1,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}g(\cdot,\cdot)*x&amp;=x,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}g(\cdot,\cdot)*x&amp;=x,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}g(\cdot,\cdot)*y&amp;=y,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}g(\cdot,\cdot)*y&amp;=y,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot)*1&amp;=0,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot)*1&amp;=0,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot)*x&amp;=1,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot)*x&amp;=1,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(58)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot)*y&amp;=0,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot)*y&amp;=0,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(59)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot)*1&amp;=0,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot)*1&amp;=0,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(60)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E61"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot)*x&amp;=0,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot)*x&amp;=0,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(61)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E62"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot)*y&amp;=0.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot)*y&amp;=0.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(62)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">By superposition, this property will then also
apply to any shift-adjusted input polynomial of the form
<math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{ab}(x,y)=(x-m_{x})^{a}\,(y-m_{y})^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{ab}(x,y)=(x-m_{x})^{a}\,(y-m_{y})^{b}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By further differentiating these relationsship, it follows that the
first-order non-centered Gaussian derivative response in the <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-direction
to the monomial input <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{10}(x,y)=x-m_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{10}(x,y)=x-m_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as
the first-order non-centered Gaussian derivative response in the <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-direction
to the monomial input <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{01}(x,y)=y-m_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{01}(x,y)=y-m_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>
will be equal to 1, if the centering for the
non-centered Gaussian derivative responses
is around the point <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
