<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.T3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The result of computing the responses to the monomials <math id="S2.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{00}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>00</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{00}=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.T3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.T3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<span id="S2.T3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">normalized versions</span> of the first 6 “master key filters” shown in
Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where the spatial offset <math id="S2.T3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}=M(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}=M(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|</annotation></semantics></math>
represents the spatial mean of the absolute value of each filter.
For this table, the amplitudes of the original first 6 “master key filters” have been rescaled,
to make the response equal to 1 for the monomial of <math id="S2.T3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="S2.T3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> that best matches an idealized approximation of the filter
as either a derivative in the <math id="S2.T3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-direction or the <math id="S2.T3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-direction.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.3 </span>Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.3.2 Responses to monomials for continuous Gaussian derivative operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the result of computing the
responses to the monomials <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{00}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>00</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{00}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> for the 8 “master key filters”
in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, with the
spatial offset <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> determined from the spatial mean of the
absolute value of each filter according to <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}=M(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}=M(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|</annotation></semantics></math>.
As can be seen from these results:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The responses of the filters to the constant function <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{00}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>00</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{00}=1</annotation></semantics></math> is
very close to zero for all the filters, indicating that the filter are in this
respect DC-balanced.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filters 2, 4 and 5, the largest response is obtained for the
monomial <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that these filters
respond as strongest in a way rather closely related to first-order
derivatives in the horizontal <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-direction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filters 1, 2 and 6, the largest response is obtained for the
monomial <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that these filters
respond as strongest in a way rather closely related to first-order
derivatives in the vertical <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-direction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Filters 1–6, which approximate first-order derivatives in
either the horizontal <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-direction or the vertical <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-direction,
are, however, not normalized to return derivative responses
to the functions <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>,
to be equal to one in the respective cases of matching
orders between the monomials and the derivative approximations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filters 7 and 8, which have the qualitatively similar visual
appearance as a discrete Gaussian kernel, or a discrete delta
function with a subtracted Laplacian-of-the-Gaussian, the DC-balancing
of the “master key filters” is, however, not fully consistent with
discrete Gaussian models of those filters, since the both the
continuous integral of the continuous Gaussian kernel
and the discrete sum of the filter coefficients in discrete approximations
of Gaussian kernels should be equal to 1</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 2.3.2 Responses to monomials for continuous Gaussian derivative operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the result of
an extension of this analysis, where we have for the first 6 “master key filters”
normalized the amplitudes of the receptive fields to
make the response equal to 1 for the monomial of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{10}=x-m_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{01}=y-m_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> that best matches an idealized approximation of the filter
as either a derivative in the <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-direction or the <math id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-direction:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E63"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{1,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{1}(m,n)}{h_{1}(\cdot,\cdot)*(y-m_{y})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{1,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{1}(m,n)}{h_{1}(\cdot,\cdot)*(y-m_{y})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(63)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E64"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{2,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{2}(m,n)}{h_{2}(\cdot,\cdot)*(x-m_{x})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{2,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{2}(m,n)}{h_{2}(\cdot,\cdot)*(x-m_{x})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(64)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E65"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{3,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{3}(m,n)}{h_{3}(\cdot,\cdot)*(y-m_{y})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{3,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{3}(m,n)}{h_{3}(\cdot,\cdot)*(y-m_{y})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(65)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E66"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{4,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{4}(m,n)}{h_{4}(\cdot,\cdot)*(x-m_{x})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{4,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{4}(m,n)}{h_{4}(\cdot,\cdot)*(x-m_{x})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(66)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E67"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{5,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{5}(m,n)}{h_{5}(\cdot,\cdot)*(x-m_{x})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{5,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{5}(m,n)}{h_{5}(\cdot,\cdot)*(x-m_{x})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(67)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E68"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{6,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{6}(m,n)}{h_{6}(\cdot,\cdot)*(y-m_{y})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}}.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{6,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{6}(m,n)}{h_{6}(\cdot,\cdot)*(y-m_{y})|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(68)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">As can be seen from these results:</p>
<ul id="S2.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The complementary response to a first-order monomial in the direction
orthogonal to the preferred direction of the receptive field is comparably
rather low (of the order of <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5~\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" mathsize="0.800em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5~\%</annotation></semantics></math> or below) for Filters 1, 4, 5 and 6.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this respect, the responses to first-order monomials is rather well
consistent as approximations to first-order derivative operators for
Filters 1, 4, 5 and 6.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filter 2, the response to a first-order monomial in the direction
orthogonal to the preferred direction of the receptive field is
notable (of the order of <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12~\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" mathsize="0.800em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12~\%</annotation></semantics></math>) for Filter 3, and very notable
(of the order of <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30~\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" mathsize="0.800em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30~\%</annotation></semantics></math>) for Filter 2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this respect, there are notable deviations from approximations to
first-order derivative operators for Filter 3 and specifically for Filter 2.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">To conclude, Filters 1–6 show qualitatively rather good or very good structural
similarities to first-order derivative approximation filters along the
Cartesian coordinate direction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The matches to corresponding fully idealized models in terms of
Gaussian derivatives or discrete approximations thereof is,
however, far from perfect. A possible source to deviations from
the idealized theory could
also be because of from variations in the background periphery
of the learned filters, which, by a lack of symmetry in those spurious
variations, could contribute significantly to the
responses to the first-order monomials.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As previously remarked, for Filters 7 and 8, the estimates of the spatial
extents of the filters will be significantly biased, because of the lack of
a determination of the DC-level of these filters to correspond to a
background level of about zero. Instead, as we saw from the experimental
results in Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.3.2 Responses to monomials for continuous Gaussian derivative operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the response of
these filters to the constant function <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{00}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>00</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{00}=1</annotation></semantics></math> is very close to zero,
as opposed to being close to one, as it should be for a continuous Gaussian
filter or a discrete approximation thereof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue, we will in the following DC-correct Filters 7 and 8
according to</p>
<table id="S2.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E69"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n;\;C_{7})=h_{7}(m,n)-C_{7},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>DC</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n;\;C_{7})=h_{7}(m,n)-C_{7},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(69)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E70"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n;\;C_{8})=h_{8}(m,n)-C_{8},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>DC</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n;\;C_{8})=h_{8}(m,n)-C_{8},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(70)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">by determining the values of the DC-correction-constants <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{8}</annotation></semantics></math>
so as to minimize the determinant of the variance-based spatial spread measure
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|)</annotation></semantics></math> according to</p>
<table id="S2.E71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C_{i}}=\operatorname{argmin}_{C_{i}}\det V(|h_{i}(\cdot,\cdot)-C_{i}|)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>argmin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo movablelimits="false">det</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C_{i}}=\operatorname{argmin}_{C_{i}}\det V(|h_{i}(\cdot,\cdot)-C_{i}|),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(71)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{7,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{7,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then further normalize the resulting DC-corrected filters according to</p>
<table id="S2.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E72"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n;\;\hat{C}_{7})}{h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\hat{C}_{7})*1|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>DC</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>DC</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n;\;\hat{C}_{7})}{h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\hat{C}_{7})*1|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(72)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E73"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n;\;\hat{C}_{8})}{h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\hat{C}_{8})*1|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>DC</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>DC</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>8</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)=\frac{h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n;\;\hat{C}_{8})}{h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\hat{C}_{8})*1|_{(x,y)^{T}=(0,0)^{T}}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(73)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">so that we obtain the following desirable properties</p>
<table id="S2.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E74"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)\star 1=1,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)\star 1=1,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(74)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E75"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)\star 1=1,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)\star 1=1,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(75)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.5" class="ltx_p">in analogy to corresponding relationships for continuous Gaussian kernels
and appropriate discrete approximations thereof.</p>
</div>
