<span id="S2.T5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">normalized versions</span> of the last 2 “master key filters” shown in
Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where the spatial offset <math id="S2.T5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}=M(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{x},m_{y})^{T}=M(|h(\cdot,\cdot)|</annotation></semantics></math>
represents the spatial mean of the absolute value of each filter.
For this table, the amplitudes of the original last 2 “master key filters” have been rescaled,
to make the response equal to 1 for the monomial of <math id="S2.T5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{00}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>00</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{00}=1</annotation></semantics></math>
equal to 1.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> show graphs of the error measure</p>
<table id="S2.E76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\det V(|h_{i}(\cdot,\cdot)-C_{i}|)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\det V(|h_{i}(\cdot,\cdot)-C_{i}|)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(76)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">according to
(<a href="#S2.E71" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">71</span></a>) for determining
the DC compensation constants for <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C_{7}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C_{7}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C_{8}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C_{8}}</annotation></semantics></math>
in this way.
Table <a href="#S2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the result of computing the spatial spread measures
for the resulting DC-compensated filters
<math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>DC</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>DC</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{DC}}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>
according to (<a href="#S2.E69" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">69</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E70" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">70</span></a>).
As can be seen from the results,
the use of DC compensation substantially reduces the variance-based spatial spread
measures for Filters 7 and 8, compared to the variance-based spread measures
of the corresponding not DC-compensated filters shown in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1.3 Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order pure difference operators ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> additionally shows the responses to monomials
up to order 1 for the resulting DC-compensated and
<math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-normalized filters <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>
according to (<a href="#S2.E72" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">72</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E73" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">73</span></a>).
As can be seen from the table, for the larger-size Gaussian-like
Filter 8, the responses to the first-order monomials <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>
are rather low. For the smaller-size Gaussian-like
Filter 7, the deviations of those responses from the ideal
value 0 are, however, notable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows a visualization of
the resulting normalized versions of the 8 “master key filters”,
which we will then approximate by idealized receptive field models in
different ways in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Before proceeding to the modelling stage, we will, however,
first in the next section introduce a way of defining spatial
spread measures, that are less influenced by spurious variabilities in
the peripheries of the learned filters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variances, by windowed estimates of the spatial spread measures</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to spurious variations in the background of the learned filters,
these spurious variations may lead to substantial biases in the
mean-based and variance-based spread measures, because
those spurious variations are weighted with either a linear
or a quadratic factor in the first- and second-order moments
that define the spatial spread measures. This situation is, thus,
in contrast to previous use of such spatial spread measures
in Lindeberg (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025</a></cite>)
for characterizing properties of idealized mathematical
models of receptive fields with regard to variabilities in their
spatial extent under variations of the spatial scale parameter.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.5.1 </span>Weighted spatial spread measures</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce this substantial source to bias, we will in this section
replace the previous uniformly weighted first- and second-order
moments by weighted first- and second-order moments,
with the weight functions determined from discrete approximations
of Gaussian functions with similar values of the scale parameters
as we would obtain by estimating the scale parameters of
idealized filter models from the spatial variances of the corresponding
discrete kernels.
Thus, we define weighted spatial mean values and spatial variance
of continuous or discrete filter kernels <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> according to:</p>
<table id="S2.E77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))=\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))=\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,g(p)\,dp},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(77)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.E78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))=\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,p^{T}\,h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}\\
-\left(\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))=\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,p^{T}\,h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}\\
-\left(\frac{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}{\int_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,g(p)\,dp}\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(78)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">over a continuous image domain and according to</p>
<table id="S2.E79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{T(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))=\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,T(p)\,dp}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,T(p)\,dp}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{T(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))=\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,T(p)\,dp}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,T(p)\,dp},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(79)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.E80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{T(\cdot)}((h(\cdot))=\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,p^{T}\,h(p)\,T(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,T(p)}\\
-\left(\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,T(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,T(p)}\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{T(\cdot)}((h(\cdot))=\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,p^{T}\,h(p)\,T(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,T(p)}\\
-\left(\frac{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}p\,h(p)\,T(p)}{\sum_{p\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2}}h(p)\,T(p)}\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(80)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">over a discrete image domain, with the intention that peripheral values
corresponding to spurious structures in the learned filters should
have lower influence on the spatial spread measures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, if the continuous receptive field <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> would be equal to the
continuous weighting filter <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the weighted spatial mean value
and the weighted spatial variance would correspond to mean value
and standard deviation in <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm.
Similarly, if the discrete receptive field <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> would be equal to the
discrete weighting filter <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the weighted spatial mean value
and the weighted spatial variance would correspond to mean value
and standard deviation in <math id="S2.SS5.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm.
For our purpose, we do other hand formulate a hybrid approach,
where we use the desired shape of the filter model as a prior
to suppress the influence of spurious responses in the learned
filter spatial positions in the peripheries of the support regions
of the learned filters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.5.2 </span>Determining idealized shapes for the weighting kernels from the qualitative shapes of the “master key filters”</h4>

<div id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The intention is then to choose either continuous or discrete weighting
filters that match the qualitative shapes of the filters to be modelled
in the following ways:</p>
<ul id="S2.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filter 8 we will either use anisotropic
continuous Cartesian-aligned anisotropic Gaussian kernels</p>
<table id="S2.E81" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x,y;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(y;\;\sigma_{y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x,y;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(y;\;\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(81)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">or discrete Cartesian-aligned anisotropic Gaussian kernels</p>
<table id="S2.E82" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(m;\;\sigma_{x})\,T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(n;\;\sigma_{y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(m;\;\sigma_{x})\,T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(n;\;\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(82)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">having possibly different
values of the scale parameters <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>
in the <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>- and <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-directions, respectively.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filters 2, 4 and 5, we will either use the absolute value of
either a first-order horizontal <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-derivative
of a continuous Gaussian kernel</p>
<table id="S2.E83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D},x}(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(y;\;\sigma_{y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mtext>1D</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D},x}(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(y;\;\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(83)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">or the absolute value of a discrete approximation of a horizontal <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-derivative of a Gaussian kernel</p>
<table id="S2.E84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta_{x}T)(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=(\Delta_{x}T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}})(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(y;\;\sigma_{y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta_{x}T)(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=(\Delta_{x}T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}})(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(y;\;\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(84)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for appropriately matched difference
operators
<math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{x}\in\{\delta_{x-},\delta_{x+},\delta_{x}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{x}\in\{\delta_{x-},\delta_{x+},\delta_{x}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
in the horizontal <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-direction, with the scale parameters <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I4.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>
determined from variance-based spatial spread measures
of the corresponding filters.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filters 1, 3 and 6, we will either use the absolute value of a first-order vertical <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-derivative
of continuous Gaussian kernel</p>
<table id="S2.E85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D},y}(y;\;\sigma_{y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mtext>1D</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,g_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D},y}(y;\;\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(85)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">or the absolute value of a discrete approximation of a vertical <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-derivative of a Gaussian kernel</p>
<table id="S2.E86" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta_{y}T)(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,(\Delta_{y}T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}})(y;\;\sigma_{y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>1D</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta_{y}T)(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})=T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}}(x;\;\sigma_{x})\,(\Delta_{y}T_{\scriptsize\mbox{1D}})(y;\;\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(86)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for a appropriately matched difference operators
<math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{y}\in\{\delta_{y+},\delta_{y-},\delta_{y}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{y}\in\{\delta_{y+},\delta_{y-},\delta_{y}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
in the vertical <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-direction, with the scale parameters <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I4.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>
determined from variance-based spatial spread measures
of the corresponding filters.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS5.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The motivation for these choices is to use qualitatively similar
shapes for the spatial weighting functions in the spatial spread
measures as the absolute values of the corresponding
idealized models of receptive field shapes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6 </span>Estimating variance parameters of receptive fields from weighted spatial spread measures</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When using non-uniform weighting kernels when
computing the spatial spread measures,
the resulting variance-based measures will, however,
be affected by the spatial extent of the weighting kernels.
In the continuous case, we specifically have the
following relations:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E87"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}M_{g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})}(g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y}))=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}M_{g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})}(g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y}))=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(87)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E88"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}V_{g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})}(g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y}))=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\sigma_{x}^{2}/2&amp;0\\
0&amp;\sigma_{y}^{2}/2\end{array}\right),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}V_{g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})}(g(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y}))=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\sigma_{x}^{2}/2&amp;0\\
0&amp;\sigma_{y}^{2}/2\end{array}\right),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(88)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E89"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}M_{|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}M_{|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(89)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E90"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}V_{|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}3\,\sigma_{x}^{2}/2&amp;0\\
0&amp;\sigma_{y}^{2}/2\end{array}\right),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}V_{|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{x}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}3\,\sigma_{x}^{2}/2&amp;0\\
0&amp;\sigma_{y}^{2}/2\end{array}\right),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(90)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E91"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E91.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}M_{|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}M_{|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}0\\
0\end{array}\right),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(91)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E92"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}V_{|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\sigma_{x}^{2}/2&amp;0\\
0&amp;3\,\sigma_{y}^{2}/2\end{array}\right).\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}V_{|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{y}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\sigma_{x}^{2}/2&amp;0\\
0&amp;3\,\sigma_{y}^{2}/2\end{array}\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(92)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Our intention is then to use these expressions for the weighted
variance-based spatial spread measures for estimating the scale
parameters <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> in idealized models of the
learned “master key filters”, based on weighted variance measurements
for the learned filters.</p>
</div>
