<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="S2.T6.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The result of computing the discrete <span id="S2.T6.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weighted</span> spatial mean value <math id="S2.T6.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{T(\cdot,\cdot)}(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{T(\cdot,\cdot)}(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math> according to
Equation (<a href="#S2.E79" title="In 2.5.1 Weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2.5 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variances, by windowed estimates of the spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">79</span></a>) and the
discrete <span id="S2.T6.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weighted</span> spatial variance <math id="S2.T6.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{T(\cdot,\cdot)}(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{T(\cdot,\cdot)}(|h(\cdot,\cdot))|</annotation></semantics></math>
according to Equation (<a href="#S2.E80" title="In 2.5.1 Weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2.5 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variances, by windowed estimates of the spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">80</span></a>)
for the normalized versions of the 8 “master key filters”
according to (<a href="#S2.E63" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E68" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>)
and (<a href="#S2.E73" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">73</span></a>) when using matching shapes of
the spatial weighting filters <math id="S2.T6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to the qualitative shapes of the
“master key filters” according to
Section <a href="#S2.SS5.SSS2" title="2.5.2 Determining idealized shapes for the weighting kernels from the qualitative shapes of the “master key filters” ‣ 2.5 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variances, by windowed estimates of the spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5.2</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 2.6 Estimating variance parameters of receptive fields from weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
shows weighted spatial spread measures computed
in this way, with the weighting functions for the respective
normalized versions of the “master key filters” according
to (<a href="#S2.E63" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E68" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>)
and (<a href="#S2.E73" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">73</span></a>)
determined from the absolute values of the corresponding
idealized filter models according to
(<a href="#S3.E93" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E100" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">100</span></a>),
with the scale parameters of the weighting functions
set to <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}=\sigma_{y}=\sigma_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}=\sigma_{y}=\sigma_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> in the spatial
weighting kernels for all the “master key filters”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As can be seen from a comparison with the corresponding
non-weighted spatial spread measures in
Tables <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1.3 Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order pure difference operators ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
and <a href="#S2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>,
the use of spatial weighting in the computation of
the variance-based spread measures lead to substantially
lower estimates of the corresponding scale parameters,
and thus to a substantial decrease of the previous bias,
caused by spurious variations in the peripheries
of the learned filters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We can also see that the elements of the weighted mean value vectors are,
as for the previously computed non-weighted mean values,
either around <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pm 1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1/2</annotation></semantics></math> or 0, although again with slight
offsets from the ideal values by up to 0.1 or 0.2 grid units.
In this respect, both the weighted and the unweighted mean value
give rise to qualitatively similar results, indicating that the
spurious variations in the backgrounds of the “master key filters”
seem to have roughly symmetric statistical properties under
mirroring through the coordinate axes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we will use the modelling and analysis results from
the previous section, for modelling the 8 “master key filters”
in terms of idealized scale-space operations.
Specifically, we will explore the implications of using different
types of modelling criteria and analyze how they give rise to
different sets of scale parameters in the idealized filter models.
Overall, we will either consider separate modelling of the
different filters of the following forms:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E93"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{1,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})=(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1}),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{1,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})=(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(93)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E94"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{2,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{y,2})=(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{y,2}),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{2,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{y,2})=(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{y,2}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(94)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E95"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{3,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{y,3})=(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{y,3}),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{3,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{y,3})=(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{y,3}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(95)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E96"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{4,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{y,4})=(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{y,4}),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{4,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{y,4})=(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{y,4}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(96)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E97"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{5,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{y,5})=(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{y,5}),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{5,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{y,5})=(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{y,5}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(97)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E98"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{6,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{y,6})=(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{y,6}),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{6,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{y,6})=(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{y,6}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(98)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E99"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,7},\sigma_{y,7})=1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7}),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,7},\sigma_{y,7})=1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(99)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E100"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{y,8})=T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{y,8}),\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}h_{8,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{y,8})=T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{y,8}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(100)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> denoting a 2-D discrete
Gaussian kernel with possibly different scale parameters
<math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> in the two coordinate directions
according to (<a href="#S2.E82" title="In 1st item ‣ 2.5.2 Determining idealized shapes for the weighting kernels from the qualitative shapes of the “master key filters” ‣ 2.5 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variances, by windowed estimates of the spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a>), and for the
special case of Filter 7, the operator <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{5}^{2}=\delta_{xx}+\delta_{yy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{5}^{2}=\delta_{xx}+\delta_{yy}</annotation></semantics></math>
denoting a discrete approximation of the Laplacian operator,
and <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{7}\in{\mathbb{R}}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{7}\in{\mathbb{R}}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting a constant to be determined.
To keep the dimensionality of the search space down,
as well as for theoretical reasons, we have here stated an <span id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a priori</span>
spatially isotropic model for Filter 7 with <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x,7}=\sigma_{y,7}=\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x,7}=\sigma_{y,7}=\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The overall form of modelling above is based on the canonical model for spatial receptive fields
of the form (Lindeberg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">2021a</a></cite> Equation (23))</p>
<table id="S3.E101" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{\varphi}^{m_{1}}{\bot{\varphi}}^{m_{2}}}(x,y;\;s,\Sigma)=\partial_{\varphi}^{m_{1}}\partial_{\bot\varphi}^{m_{2}}\left(g(x,y;\;s\Sigma)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊥</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>φ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊥</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{\varphi}^{m_{1}}{\bot{\varphi}}^{m_{2}}}(x,y;\;s,\Sigma)=\partial_{\varphi}^{m_{1}}\partial_{\bot\varphi}^{m_{2}}\left(g(x,y;\;s\Sigma)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(101)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">while here (i) restricting the spatial covariance matrix <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
in the affine Gaussian kernel <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x,y;\;s\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x,y;\;s\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>
to a diagonal matrix according to
<math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{i}=\operatorname{diag}(\sigma_{x,i}^{2},\sigma_{y,i}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{i}=\operatorname{diag}(\sigma_{x,i}^{2},\sigma_{y,i}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> for each filter <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
and (ii) restricting the directional derivative
operators <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\bot\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊥</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\bot\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> to
Cartesian partial derivatives <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>,
since the spatial shapes of the
learned receptive fields appear to be very much aligned
with the Cartesian coordinate directions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we also make use of the fact that the discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel
in (<a href="#S2.E82" title="In 1st item ‣ 2.5.2 Determining idealized shapes for the weighting kernels from the qualitative shapes of the “master key filters” ‣ 2.5 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variances, by windowed estimates of the spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E9" title="In 2.1.1 Spatial spread measures applied to zero-order scale-space kernels ‣ 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) constitutes a canonical way to transfer the scale-space properties of the continuous Gaussian kernel to a discrete domain. The use of complementary centered or non-centered difference operators in (<a href="#S3.E93" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E100" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">100</span></a>) is based on the property that such difference operators applied to a discrete scale-space representation generated by convolution with the discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel preserve discrete scale-space properties (Lindeberg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">1993a</a></cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">One could, however, also conceive using other discrete approximations of Gaussian derivative operators, such as those in (Lindeberg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">2024</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeyear"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">2025</a></cite>)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the following Sections <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>–<a href="#S3.SS6" title="3.6 Method D2: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>
we will consider different ways of determining the scale
parameters <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y,i}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>, while we will then in
Section <a href="#S3.SS7" title="3.7 Two-parameter modelling of Filter 7 as a sharpening filter ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> consider
a different methodology with one more parameter for determining the parameters
<math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> in an idealized model of Filter 7.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Note:</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the visualizations for the resulting Methods A–B to be
presented, we will, however, not develop specific variance-based
methods for Filter 7, since the idealized model of that filter also
comprises an amplitude scaling factor <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> for the contribution from the
negative Laplacian-of-the-Gaussian. For this reason, we will
for Methods A–B instead interweave visualizations of
idealized models of Filter 7 based on the either <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm-based
or <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm-based Methods D2 and C2, to
be described in Section <a href="#S3.SS7" title="3.7 Two-parameter modelling of Filter 7 as a sharpening filter ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T7" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T7.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
