<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 7</span>: </span><span id="S3.T7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Estimates of the horizontal and vertical scale parameters
<math id="S3.T7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.890em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{y,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.890em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{y,i}</annotation></semantics></math>
computed according to <span id="S3.T7.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Method A</span>, based on estimates from
continuous Gaussian derivatives according to
(<a href="#S3.E103" title="In 3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">103</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E116" title="In 3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">116</span></a>)
in idealized models of the “master key filters” according
to (<a href="#S3.E93" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E100" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">100</span></a>), in turn
based on weighted measurements of the variance of the absolute
values of the learned filters according to
Table <a href="#S2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 2.6 Estimating variance parameters of receptive fields from weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A straightforward way of estimating the scale parameters
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y,i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the idealized models
(<a href="#S3.E93" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E100" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">100</span></a>) is
by making use of the closed-form expressions for the
weighted variances of the corresponding continuous
Gaussian derivative kernels according to
(<a href="#S2.E88" title="In 2.6 Estimating variance parameters of receptive fields from weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">88</span></a>),
(<a href="#S2.E90" title="In 2.6 Estimating variance parameters of receptive fields from weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">90</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S2.E92" title="In 2.6 Estimating variance parameters of receptive fields from weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">92</span></a>).
Denoting the weighted variance matrix for each
continuous kernel by</p>
<table id="S3.E102" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{|g_{\alpha}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{\alpha}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}v_{xx}&amp;v_{xy}\\
v_{xy}&amp;v_{yy}\end{array}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{|g_{\alpha}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|}(|g_{\alpha}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x},\sigma_{y})|)=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}v_{xx}&amp;v_{xy}\\
v_{xy}&amp;v_{yy}\end{array}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(102)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">we then obtain the following estimates</p>
<table id="S3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E103"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\hat{\sigma}_{x,1}=\sqrt{2\,v_{xx,1}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.130em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\hat{\sigma}_{x,1}=\sqrt{2\,v_{xx,1}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(103)</span></td></tr></tbody>
