<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S3.F7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Idealized models of the 8 “master key filters” in
Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, in terms of combinations pure
difference operators with spatial smoothing filters based on the
discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel, with the amount of
spatial smoothing determined from the spatial extents of the 8
“master key filters” using <span id="S3.F7.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method B</span> in
Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Method B: Matching of discrete spatial variances between the “master key filters” and the spatial variances of discrete derivative approximation filters, with individual scale parameters for each filter ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, based on <span id="S3.F7.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">requiring
the horizontal vs. the vertical discrete weighted
variance-based spatial spread measures for the idealized
receptive field models to be equal to the
weighted discrete spatial spread measures for
the corresponding learned filters</span>.
(Idealized 1): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 2): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{y,2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{y,2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 3): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{y,3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{y,3})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 4): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{y,4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{y,4})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 5): Centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{y,5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{y,5})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 6): Centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{y,6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{y,6})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 7): Sharpening operation
<math id="S3.F7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 8): Pure spatial smoothing
<math id="S3.F7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{y,8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{y,8})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(The numerical values of the scale parameters <math id="S3.F7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.F7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y,i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.F7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> have been determined according
to Table <a href="#S3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 3.2 Method B: Matching of discrete spatial variances between the “master key filters” and the spatial variances of discrete derivative approximation filters, with individual scale parameters for each filter ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>
and with <math id="S3.F7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> additionally according to
Table <a href="#S3.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 3.6 Method D2: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.)
(Note that the contrasts of Filters 2 and 3 are reversed in
relation to the sign conventions for corresponding “master key filters”.)</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Method B: Matching of discrete spatial variances between
the “master key filters” and the spatial variances of discrete
derivative approximation filters, with individual scale parameters
for each filter</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A possible limitation of the above computed estimates of the spatial
scale parameters from weighted variances of the discrete filters,
is that the relationship between the weighted variances of the
filters is based on a continuous model in terms of Gaussian
derivatives (Equations (<a href="#S2.E88" title="In 2.6 Estimating variance parameters of receptive fields from weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">88</span></a>),
(<a href="#S2.E90" title="In 2.6 Estimating variance parameters of receptive fields from weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">90</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S2.E92" title="In 2.6 Estimating variance parameters of receptive fields from weighted spatial spread measures ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">92</span></a>) for
continuous Gaussian derivatives, that lead to
the estimates in
Equations (<a href="#S3.E103" title="In 3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">103</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E116" title="In 3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">116</span></a>)),
while the filters that we model are genuinely discrete,
and specifically corresponding to rather fine scale levels,
where discretization effects in relation to the discrete <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vs.</span> continuous models may therefore be significant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address that issue, we will in this section instead consider a
fully discrete approach, where discrete weighted variances of fully
discrete models of the idealized receptive fields are matched to
discretely computed weighted variances of the learned filters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Denoting the operators that extract the diagonal elements
in the discrete weighted variance-based spatial spread
measure <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{T(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{T(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 Spatial spread measures for characterizing the spatial extent and the spatial offset of receptive fields ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)
by <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{xx,T(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{xx,T(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{yy,T(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{yy,T(\cdot)}(h(\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, we will in
the following determine the scale estimates in idealized
models of the “master key filters” according to
(<a href="#S3.E93" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E100" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">100</span></a>),
based on the criterion that the differences between
the diagonal elements of the variance-based spatial
spread measures should be as small as possible,
between the idealized receptive field models and
the learned filters, as specified in
Equations (<a href="#S3.E117" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">117</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E118" title="In Figure 6 ‣ 3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">118</span></a>)
in Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
