<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T8.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 8</span>: </span><span id="S3.T8.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Estimates of the horizontal and vertical scale parameters
<math id="S3.T8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.890em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{y,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.890em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{y,i}</annotation></semantics></math>
computed according to <span id="S3.T8.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Method B</span>, based on requiring
the horizontal <span id="S3.T8.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vs.</span> the vertical discrete weighted
variance-based spatial spread measures for the idealized
models of the “master key filters” to be equal to the
weighted discrete spatial spread measures for the
the corresponding learned filters.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 3.2 Method B: Matching of discrete spatial variances between the “master key filters” and the spatial variances of discrete derivative approximation filters, with individual scale parameters for each filter ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows the result of computing horizontal
and vertical scale estimates <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}</annotation></semantics></math> in this way for the 8 different
“master key filters”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 3.1 Method A: Direct transfer of scale values from spatial variances to idealized scale-space operations with different amounts of smoothing in the different coordinate directions with use of continuous Gaussian derivative model ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows visualizations
of the corresponding idealized receptive field models of these filters.</p>
</div>
