<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 9</span>: </span><span id="S3.T9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Estimates of the horizontal and vertical scale parameters
<math id="S3.T9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.890em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{x,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{y,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.890em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{y,i}</annotation></semantics></math>
computed according to <span id="S3.T9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Method C1</span>, based on minimizing
the discrete <math id="S3.T9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm between the idealized receptive field
model <math id="S3.T9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext mathsize="1.110em">ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})</annotation></semantics></math>
according to (<a href="#S3.E93" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E100" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">100</span></a>)
and the normalized learned filter
<math id="S3.T9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext mathsize="1.110em">norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> according to
(<a href="#S2.E63" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E68" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S2.E73" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">73</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Method C1: <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm-based fitting to idealized
model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters
along the horizontal and the vertical directions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the above spatial spread-measure based modelling operations
aim at reflecting the spatial extents of the receptive fields, a
conceptual problem arises if there is a mismatch between the shapes
of the filters in the idealized receptive fields to the shapes of the
learned receptive fields.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will address this issue by instead fitting the
idealized receptive field models to the learned receptive fields
in discrete <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm. Thus, we will estimate the scale parameters
of the receptive fields in the following way:</p>
<table id="S3.E119" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E119.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\sigma}_{x,i},\hat{\sigma}_{y,i})=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\hat{\sigma}_{x,i},\hat{\sigma}_{y,i})}\|\Delta h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,i},\sigma_{y,i})\|_{1}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\hat{\sigma}_{x,i},\hat{\sigma}_{y,i})}\|h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,i},\sigma_{y,i})-h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\sigma}_{x,i},\hat{\sigma}_{y,i})=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\hat{\sigma}_{x,i},\hat{\sigma}_{y,i})}\|\Delta h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,i},\sigma_{y,i})\|_{1}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\hat{\sigma}_{x,i},\hat{\sigma}_{y,i})}\|h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,i},\sigma_{y,i})-h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(119)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with the idealized receptive field models
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})</annotation></semantics></math>
according to (<a href="#S3.E93" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E100" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">100</span></a>)
and the normalized “master key filters”
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> according to
(<a href="#S2.E63" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E68" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S2.E73" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">73</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 3.2 Method B: Matching of discrete spatial variances between the “master key filters” and the spatial variances of discrete derivative approximation filters, with individual scale parameters for each filter ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows the resulting
horizontal and vertical scale estimates obtained in this way.
Figure <a href="#S3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 3.2 Method B: Matching of discrete spatial variances between the “master key filters” and the spatial variances of discrete derivative approximation filters, with individual scale parameters for each filter ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows visualizations of
the corresponding idealized receptive fields.</p>
</div>
