<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="S3.F9.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Idealized models of the 8 “master key filters” in
Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, in terms of combinations pure
difference operators with spatial smoothing filters based on the
discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel, with the amount of
spatial smoothing determined from the spatial extents of the 8
“master key filters” using <span id="S3.F9.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method C2</span> in
Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Method C2: 𝑙_1-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, based on <span id="S3.F9.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimizing the
discrete <math id="S3.F9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm between the idealized receptive field models
and the normalized versions of the learned filters</span> according to
(<a href="#S3.E120" title="In 3.4 Method C2: 𝑙_1-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">120</span></a>).
(Idealized 1): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{1},\sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{1},\sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 2): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{2},\sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{2},\sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 3): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{3},\sigma_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{3},\sigma_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 4): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{4},\sigma_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{4},\sigma_{4})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 5): Centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{5},\sigma_{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{5},\sigma_{5})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 6): Centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{6},\sigma_{6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{6},\sigma_{6})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 7): Sharpening operation
<math id="S3.F9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 8): Pure spatial smoothing
<math id="S3.F9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(m,n;\;\sigma_{8},\sigma_{8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(m,n;\;\sigma_{8},\sigma_{8})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(The numerical values of <math id="S3.F9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S3.F9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> are according to
Table <a href="#S3.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ 3.3 Method C1: 𝑙_1-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>,
and with <math id="S3.F9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>
additionally according to Table <a href="#S3.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 3.6 Method D2: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.)
(Note that the contrasts of Filters 2 and 3 are reversed in
relation to the sign conventions for corresponding “master key filters”.)</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Method C2: <math id="S3.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm-based fitting to idealized
model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter
along the horizontal and the vertical directions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a complement to the above analysis, let us also consider the
special case when scale parameters are required to be the
same in the horizontal and the vertical directions
<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x,i}=\sigma_{y,i}=\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x,i}=\sigma_{y,i}=\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, for which we then
determine each scale estimate <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from the criterion</p>
<table id="S3.E120" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E120.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{i}=\operatorname{argmin}_{\hat{\sigma}_{i}}\|\Delta h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{i},\sigma_{i})\|_{1}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{\sigma_{i}}\|h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{i},\sigma_{i})-h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{i}=\operatorname{argmin}_{\hat{\sigma}_{i}}\|\Delta h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{i},\sigma_{i})\|_{1}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{\sigma_{i}}\|h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{i},\sigma_{i})-h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(120)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ 3.3 Method C1: 𝑙_1-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows the result
of computing scale estimates for the idealized receptive field models
in this way.
Figure <a href="#S3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 3.3 Method C1: 𝑙_1-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows visualizations
of the resulting receptive fields.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S3.F10.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
