<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F10.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="S3.F10.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Idealized models of the 8 “master key filters” in
Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, in terms of combinations pure
difference operators with spatial smoothing filters based on the
discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel, with the amount of
spatial smoothing determined from the spatial extents of the 8
“master key filters” using <span id="S3.F10.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method D1</span> in
Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Method D1: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, based on <span id="S3.F10.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimizing the
discrete <math id="S3.F10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm between the idealized receptive field models
and the normalized versions of the learned filters and using
different values of the scale parameters in the horizontal and the
vertical directions</span> according to
(<a href="#S3.E121" title="In 3.5 Method D1: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">121</span></a>).
(Idealized 1): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{x,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{x,1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 2): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{x,2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,2},\sigma_{x,2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 3): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{x,3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,3},\sigma_{x,3})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 4): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{x,4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,4},\sigma_{x,4})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 5): Centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{x,5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,5},\sigma_{x,5})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 6): Centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{x,6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,6},\sigma_{x,6})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 7): Sharpening operation
<math id="S3.F10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 8): Pure spatial smoothing
<math id="S3.F10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{x,8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(m,n;\;\sigma_{x,8},\sigma_{x,8})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(The numerical values of <math id="S3.F10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.F10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> are according to
Table <a href="#S3.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ 3.4 Method C2: 𝑙_1-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, and with <math id="S3.F10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>
additionally according to Table <a href="#S3.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 3.6 Method D2: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.)
(Note that the contrasts of Filters 2 and 3 are reversed in
relation to the sign conventions for corresponding “master key filters”.)</span></figcaption>
</figure>
