<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T12.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 12</span>: </span><span id="S3.T12.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Estimates of the joint horizontal and vertical scale parameters
<math id="S3.T12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.890em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> computed according to <span id="S3.T12.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Method D2</span>, based on minimizing
the discrete <math id="S3.T12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm between the idealized receptive field
model <math id="S3.T12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext mathsize="1.110em">ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{x,1},\sigma_{y,1})</annotation></semantics></math>
according to (<a href="#S3.E93" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S3.E100" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">100</span></a>)
and the normalized learned filter
<math id="S3.T12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext mathsize="1.110em">norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> according to
(<a href="#S2.E63" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>)–(<a href="#S2.E68" title="In 2.3.3 Responses to monomials for the 8 “master key filters” operators ‣ 2.3 Measuring the accuracy in relation to centered or non-centered difference operators combined with spatial smoothing ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S2.E73" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">73</span></a>), under the assumption
that the values of the horizontal and the vertical scale
parameters are the same <math id="S3.T12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x,i}=\sigma_{y,i}=\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x,i}=\sigma_{y,i}=\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
