<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F11.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="S3.F11.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Idealized models of the 8 “master key filters” in
Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, in terms of combinations pure
difference operators with spatial smoothing filters based on the
discrete analogue of the Gaussian kernel, with the amount of
spatial smoothing determined from the spatial extents of the 8
“master key filters” using <span id="S3.F11.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method D2</span> in
Section <a href="#S3.SS6" title="3.6 Method D2: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, based on <span id="S3.F11.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimizing the
discrete <math id="S3.F11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm between the idealized receptive field models
and the normalized versions of the learned filters and using the
same values of the scale parameters in the horizontal and the
vertical directions</span> according to
(<a href="#S3.E120" title="In 3.4 Method C2: 𝑙_1-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">120</span></a>).
(Idealized 1): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{1},\sigma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{1},\sigma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 2): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{2},\sigma_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{2},\sigma_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 3): Non-centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{3},\sigma_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y-}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{3},\sigma_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 4): Non-centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{4},\sigma_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x+}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{4},\sigma_{4})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 5): Centered horizontal difference operator
<math id="S3.F11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{5},\sigma_{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{x}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{5},\sigma_{5})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 6): Centered vertical difference operator
<math id="S3.F11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{6},\sigma_{6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta_{y}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{6},\sigma_{6})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 7): Sharpening operation
<math id="S3.F11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\gamma_{7}\,(\nabla_{5}^{2}T)(m,n;\;\sigma_{7},\sigma_{7})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Idealized 8): Pure spatial smoothing
<math id="S3.F11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(m,n;\;\sigma_{8},\sigma_{8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(m,n;\;\sigma_{8},\sigma_{8})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(The numerical values of <math id="S3.F11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.F11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.890em" minsize="0.890em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> are according to
Table <a href="#S3.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ 3.5 Method D1: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, and with <math id="S3.F11.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>
additionally according to Table <a href="#S3.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 3.6 Method D2: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.)
(Note that the contrasts of Filters 2 and 3 are reversed in
relation to the sign conventions for corresponding “master key filters”.)</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Method D2: <math id="S3.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm-based fitting to idealized
model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter
along the horizontal and the vertical directions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a complement to the above analysis, let us also consider the
special case when scale parameters are required to be the
same in the horizontal and the vertical directions
<math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x,i}=\sigma_{y,i}=\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x,i}=\sigma_{y,i}=\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, for which we then
determine each scale estimate <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from the criterion</p>
<table id="S3.E122" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E122.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{i}=\operatorname{argmin}_{\hat{\sigma}_{i}}\|\Delta h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{i},\sigma_{i})\|_{2}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{\sigma_{i}}\|h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{i},\sigma_{i})-h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="1.140em">^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{i}=\operatorname{argmin}_{\hat{\sigma}_{i}}\|\Delta h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{i},\sigma_{i})\|_{2}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{\sigma_{i}}\|h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{i},\sigma_{i})-h_{i,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(122)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ 3.5 Method D1: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows the result
of computing scale estimates for the idealized receptive field models
in this way.
Figure <a href="#S3.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 3.5 Method D1: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> shows visualizations
of the resulting idealized receptive fields.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T13" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T13.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T13.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T13.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Norm</th>
<th id="S3.T13.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S3.T13.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\gamma}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\gamma}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T13.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T13.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T13.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.654</td>
<td id="S3.T13.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.522</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T13.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T13.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b"><math id="S3.T13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T13.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.675</td>
<td id="S3.T13.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.526</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 13</span>: </span><span id="S3.T13.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Estimates of the parameters <math id="S3.T13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> in
the sharpening filter model (<a href="#S3.E99" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">99</span></a>) of
Filter 7, computed with respect to minimizing either the
discrete <math id="S3.T13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm or the discrete <math id="S3.T13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm of the difference
between the idealized receptive field model and the normalized
filter (<a href="#S2.E72" title="In 2.4 Reducing the bias in the estimates of the spatial variance of the Gaussian-smoothing-like receptive fields by DC-compensation ‣ 2 Measuring characteristic properties of the 8 “master key filters” in terms of spatial spread measures and responses to lower-order polynomials ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">72</span></a>) according to
(<a href="#S3.E123" title="In 3.7 Two-parameter modelling of Filter 7 as a sharpening filter ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">123</span></a>) and
(<a href="#S3.E124" title="In 3.7 Two-parameter modelling of Filter 7 as a sharpening filter ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">124</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.7 </span>Two-parameter modelling of Filter 7 as a sharpening filter</h3>

<div id="S3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Filter 7, for which the idealized model in
(<a href="#S3.E99" title="In 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">99</span></a>) comprises a complementary scaling
parameter <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> in addition to the scale parameter
<math id="S3.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>, we determine these two parameters simultaneously,
by joint minimization over either the discrete <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm or
the discrete <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm according to</p>
<table id="S3.E123" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E123.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\sigma}_{7},\hat{\alpha}_{7})=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})}\|\Delta h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})\|_{1}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})}\|h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})-h_{i7\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\sigma}_{7},\hat{\alpha}_{7})=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})}\|\Delta h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})\|_{1}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})}\|h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})-h_{i7\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(123)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E124" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E124.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\sigma}_{7},\hat{\alpha}_{7})=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})}\|\Delta h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})\|_{2}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})}\|h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})-h_{i7\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>ideal</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\sigma}_{7},\hat{\alpha}_{7})=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})}\|\Delta h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})\|_{2}=\\
=\operatorname{argmin}_{(\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})}\|h_{7,\scriptsize\mbox{ideal}}(\cdot,\cdot;\;\sigma_{7},\alpha_{7})-h_{i7\scriptsize\mbox{norm}}(\cdot,\cdot)\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(124)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These methods are in the graphical illustrations above also referred
to as “Method C2” and “Method D2”, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 3.6 Method D2: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with the same scale parameter along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> shows numerical
values of the resulting parameter estimates <math id="S3.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\gamma}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\gamma}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained in this way.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Visualizations of the corresponding filter maps are given among
the visualizations of the other filter maps for Method C2 and
Method D2 in Figures <a href="#S3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 3.3 Method C1: 𝑙_1-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>
and <a href="#S3.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 3.5 Method D1: 𝑙_2-norm-based fitting to idealized model for scale-space operations with different scale parameters along the horizontal and the vertical directions ‣ 3 Modelling the 8 “master key filters” in terms of idealized scale-space operations ‣ Modelling and analysis of the 8 filters from the ”master key filters hypothesis” for depthwise-separable deep networks in relation to idealized receptive fields based on scale-space theory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
